@startuml 05-sequence-instructor
' =====================================================================
' Instructor configuration and review
' Traces to: FR-1..FR-11, NFR-9, NFR-10, DR-1..DR-3
' =====================================================================

title Instructor Configuration and Review

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 200

actor Instructor as instr
participant "Web\nFrontend" as fe #DCEFD9
participant "Web Backend\n/ REST API" as be #DCEFD9
participant "Repository\nLayer" as repo #DCEFD9
database "Grading DB" as db #DCEFD9

== Configure an assignment: grading mode and language [FR-1, FR-6] ==

instr -> fe : create assignment,\nchoose IMMEDIATE or NIGHTLY,\nchoose the language
fe -> be : POST /assignments\n{platform_assignment_id, name,\ngrading_mode, language}
be -> repo : create_assignment()
repo -> db : INSERT assignment
be --> fe : 201 {assignment_id}

note right of instr #FFF8DC
  **grading_mode is the only mode setting**, and
  it is on the assignment. IMMEDIATE grades
  during the submission run; NIGHTLY defers to a
  triggered rerun.

  There is no per-target feedback setting. Every
  registered target wants AI feedback by default,
  and the instructor's test suite opts individual
  targets out at run time by calling
  ai_not_needed. A target she never wants
  feedback on simply is not registered here.

  **The identifiers registered here are a
  contract with her test suite.** She passes
  these exact strings to ai_not_needed and
  add_test_result. Rename one and her suite
  stops matching, so the API rejects unknown
  names loudly. See AD-3.

  For the instructor guide: IMMEDIATE puts LLM
  latency inside the platform's autograder
  timeout, and submissions spike before a
  deadline. Large courses should prefer NIGHTLY.
end note

== Define grading targets [FR-2, FR-3, FR-7] ==

loop for each method or class to grade
  instr -> fe : add target\n(identifier, kind, max_score)
  fe -> be : POST /assignments/{id}/targets
  be -> repo : create_target()
  repo -> db : INSERT grading_target
end

note over instr #FFF8DC
  She does not designate source files. The AI
  phase derives the file from the target's
  identifier, its kind and the assignment's
  language (AD-12), so a pattern here would
  restate what she already typed.

  A FILE-kind target's identifier IS the glob,
  which covers the case where one target spans
  several files.

  Resolution must land on exactly one file.
  Zero or several is a feedback failure the
  student sees, so an identifier that does not
  match the assignment's layout shows up
  quickly rather than producing vague feedback
  nobody can account for.
end note

== Attach rubric and feedback guidance [FR-4, FR-5, FR-13] ==

instr -> fe : write criteria +\nfeedback guidance
fe -> be : PUT /targets/{id}/rubric
be -> repo : upsert_rubric()
repo -> db : INSERT rubric\n(version = n+1, is_current = true)
repo -> db : UPDATE prior rubric\nSET is_current = false

note right of repo #FFF8DC
  Rubrics are versioned, not overwritten.
  feedback rows reference rubric_id, so feedback
  generated last week stays explainable after an
  edit this week.
end note

== Review results [FR-8, FR-9, FR-10, NFR-10] ==

instr -> fe : open results dashboard
fe -> be : GET /assignments/{id}/results\n?filter=has_deduction
be -> repo : list_results()
repo -> db : SELECT joining submission,\nsubmission_target, test_result, ai_feedback
db --> repo : rows
be --> fe : 200 {results}
fe --> instr : submissions needing attention

instr -> fe : drill into one submission
fe -> be : GET /submissions/{id}
be -> repo : get_submission_detail()
repo -> db : SELECT test_result rows,\nai_feedback rows (every provider),\nai_needed, completed_at, last_error,\nagreement_score, score_spread
db --> repo : detail
be --> fe : 200 {detail}
fe --> instr : side-by-side provider feedback,\nconfidence, and AI status\n[FR-27, NFR-12]

note right of fe #FFF8DC
  test_result.status gives the dashboard raw
  functional pass/fail, so FR-8, FR-9 and FR-10
  are servable without deriving anything from AI
  scores.

  Three more columns tell the blank-looking
  feedback cases apart: ai_needed false means her
  suite opted the target out; completed_at NULL
  means the nightly rerun has not happened yet;
  last_error set means the attempt ran and
  failed, and the student has already been told
  so in results.json.

  Coverage depends on her calling add_test_result.
  It is optional, so a suite that never calls it
  leaves this view empty. Say so in the
  instructor guide rather than letting an empty
  dashboard look like a bug.
end note

== Rate feedback quality [FR-11] ==

instr -> fe : rate feedback (1-5 + comment)
fe -> be : POST /feedback/{id}/review
be -> repo : create_review()
repo -> db : INSERT ai_feedback_review

note over instr, db #FFF8DC
  **OQ-3, open:** can the instructor EDIT
  generated feedback before the student sees it?

  Mode-dependent. In NIGHTLY there is now a
  natural place to put an approval gate: the
  instructor approving a submission IS setting
  ai_authorized_at, which AD-11 already uses as
  the signal to grade. So "hold until approved"
  needs no new mechanism, just a different actor
  setting the same flag.

  In IMMEDIATE there is no window at all:
  feedback reaches the student in the same run
  that generates it. If review-before-release
  turns out to be required, that is an argument
  against offering IMMEDIATE, not a feature to
  add.

  If review-before-release is required, it is an
  argument against offering immediate mode at
  all. Decide before Student B builds the
  dashboard.
end note

@enduml
